# Line endings.
#
# `.editorconfig` says `end_of_line = lf`, and `dotnet format whitespace
# --verify-no-changes` enforces it in CI. But git had nothing to say on the
# subject, so with `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default — a clone rewrote
# every file to CRLF on the way out. CI runs on Linux and never noticed; a
# developer on Windows got a format gate that failed on files they had not
# touched, and every `git add` printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF".
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository *and* on checkout, on
# every platform. Binary files are still detected and left alone by `auto`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exceptions: Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF, because cmd.exe and
# PowerShell's older parser can misread a lone LF in a batch file.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Declared binary so git never attempts a line-ending or diff transformation on
# them. `auto` would almost certainly get these right on its own; saying so
# explicitly costs nothing and removes the "almost".
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.xlsx binary
*.snk binary
*.pfx binary

# Generated, and reviewed as output rather than as source. Marking them keeps a
# regenerated client out of language statistics and collapses it by default in a
# pull request, so the diff that matters stays visible next to it.
contracts/openapi.json linguist-generated=true
frontend/src/lib/api/generated/** linguist-generated=true
db/erd/*.md linguist-generated=true
